maria: opened a tiny docs PR clarifying how to handle 429s, timeouts, and context-trim behavior across Hosted/Dedicated. link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/1482 :wave:
kevin: thanks — skimmed. useful. please call out Dedicated's burst buckets vs Hosted rate-limits. they behave slightly differently during short spikes.
sam: also add an explicit Retry-After parsing example (lots of callers miss fractional seconds and jitter).
maria: will add JS + Python snippets, and a 2-line explanation of when to stop retrying (sustained vs transient).
alex: for SDK users let's avoid duplicating logic — include a 1-line show-and-link to the JS/Py SDK helpers and then a tiny snippet for raw HTTP clients.
lisa: FYI runtime fallbacks can hide 429s: infra may retry on a compatible model variant and return 200 with fewer tokens or a warning field. docs should list what developers actually see: 429, 504, or 200+warning.
maria: added a short table mapping (cause -> visible response): rate limit -> 429; runtime abort -> 504; context trim -> 200 + "context_trimmed": true in response payload. linked to runtime behavior section.
docs-bot: checklist — PR 1482: linkcheck, snippet lint, SDK cross-ref. assigned to @kevin and @alex for quick review. :white_check_mark:
sam: tiny nit: under "when to back off" add a one-sentence flow: spike -> short retries + jitter; sustained -> exponential backoff + alert. customers keep retrying forever.
alex: i'll draft the two-sentence guidance to match SDK best-practices and push.
maria: adding Retry-After JS snippet now:
```js
// naive Retry-After handler
const sleep = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
async function postWithRetry(fetchFn, url, body) {
  for (let i=0;i<4;i++) {
    const res = await fetchFn(url, { method: 'POST', body });
    if (res.status !== 429) return res;
    const ra = Math.max(1, Number(res.headers.get('retry-after') || 1));
    await sleep((ra + Math.random()*0.5) * 1000);
  }
  throw new Error('rate-limited');
}
```
kevin: looks good. please also add the troubleshooting runbook link for "intermittent 429s w/out console quota spike" — that helped support root-cause many times.
lisa: and note the recommended mitigation: reduce batch size + add client-side rate limiter before backing off to avoid slam.
maria: added runbook link and a short combined example (backoff + reduce-batch). i'll merge after kevin+alex thumbs. pinging @support to surface to PMs if customers ask.
